<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polemon I of Pontus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 8 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Polemon I was a Roman client king of Pontus and later of the Bosporan Kingdom in the late 1st century BCE. The son of the orator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-laodicea">Zeno of Laodicea</a>, he was made king of part of Pontus by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> in 37 BCE, kept his throne under Augustus and married Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythodoris">Pythodorida</a>. He was killed around 8 BCE while campaigning against the Aspurgians near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-azov">Sea of Azov</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Polemon I Pythodoros was an Anatolian Greek, the son and heir of Zenon, an orator and prominent aristocrat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laodicea-on-the-lycus">Laodicea on the Lycus</a>. Zenon supported the orator Hybreas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milas">Mylasa</a> when Quintus Labienus marched on that city, and in 40 BCE he urged resistance to Labienus and King Pacorus I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> during their invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Zenon was a friend and ally of the triumvir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 37 BCE Mark Antony appointed Polemon to part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, as one of several client kings who paid tribute. In 36 BCE Polemon took part in Antony's Parthian campaign and was the only man of a detachment under Oppius Statianus who was not killed; he was captured and released for a ransom. Antony then sent him to Artavasdes, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/media-atropatene">Media Atropatene</a>, to seek an alliance, and in 31 BCE rewarded him with Lesser Armenia. Plutarch lists him among the subject kings who sent troops to Antony at Actium, though he did not fight there in person. In 26 BCE Cassius Dio calls him king of Pontus and records that he was enrolled among the friends and allies of the Roman people.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>'s account suggests he gained Pontus by helping to suppress Arsaces, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnaces-ii-of-pontus">Pharnaces II</a>, who had tried to claim a throne without permission. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa</a> later sent him against Scribonius, who had taken the Bosporan Kingdom and married its regent Dynamis. The people killed Scribonius but resisted Polemon, and he could not quell the rising until Agrippa prepared a campaign at Sinope. They then surrendered, Polemon was made king, and with Augustus's support he married Dynamis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, Polemon sacked the Greek city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-river">Tanais</a> because it would not obey him and conquered Colchis. He then attacked the Aspurgiani, a Maeotian people, under a pretence of friendship, and they defeated him, took him alive and killed him. The date of his death is unknown; an inscription shows him still on the throne in 2 BCE. His second wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythodoris">Pythodorida</a>, ruled after him over Colchis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trapezus</a> and Pharnacia, and Strabo calls her wise and qualified to preside over affairs of state. Their son Zenon became king of Greater Armenia in 18 CE as Artaxias III, and their daughter Antonia Tryphaena married Cotys VIII, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polemon I of Pontus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pharnaces II, Asander and his wife Dynamis, <b>Polemon</b> I and then Aspurgus ruled with Roman approval, and during the first century CE the kings took the names Tiberius Julius, styled themselves friends of Caesar and of the Romans, and placed the emperor’s portrait on their coins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 37 BCE Mark Antony made <b>Polemon</b>, son of the orator Zeno of Laodicea, king of part of the former Kingdom of Pontus.</blockquote><a href="/state/polemonid-pontus">Polemonid Pontus</a></li></ul></section>
